  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 243 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance shows troughing moving in at 500-mb throughout the \nday Saturday, which would be associated with a surface low in \nEastern portions of Colorado. Southeasterly winds are favored to be \nin place across the area due to this surface low, with NBM guidance \nsuggesting these winds will sustain between 15 and 20 MPH, and gust \nto around 30 MPH. Surface high temperatures are currently forecast \nin the low to mid 50s in spite of the strong southeasterly flow \nexpected throughout the day. This would be driven by a 75-90% chance \nof cloud cover above 50% based on LREF guidance, alongside a strong \nsurface high in the Midwest recirculating cool continental air into \nthe region. \n\nGoing into the evening hours Saturday, the likelihood for \nprecipitation begins to increase, with PoPs (probability for \nprecipitation) into Sunday morning up around 60% in some places. NBM \n24 hr precipitation guidance shows between a 50 and 65% chance that \ngreater than 0.1 inches of precipitation falls across the forecast \narea between Saturday evening and Sunday evening. Rain is very \nlikely to be the precipitation type, as LREF guidance shows over a \n90% chance that low temperatures will not fall to freezing during \nthis time period. Additionally, all members in the 00Z run of the \nLREF clusters on November 11 show no wintry precipitation \noccurring, an improvement from recent runs. Activity is forecast\nto remain widespread rather than convective, as CAPE (a measure\nof atmospheric instability) values from the LREF show no more \nthan a few hundred J/kg in the best scenario. Highs on Sunday \nare forecast in the mid 40s to lower 50s, with no hazards \nexpected. \n\nGEFS and EC ensembles are in good agreement that ridging will be \nentering the area Monday, as troughing once again moves into the \nWestern United States off the Pacific Coast. Highs are forecast to \nreturn to around normal Monday and Tuesday (50s), with winds set to \nincrease, particularly on Tuesday, as the trough approaches. NBM \nguidance suggests that winds on Tuesday have at least a 40% chance \nof sustaining above 20 MPH and gusting above 30 MPH across nearly \nthe entire forecast region. Precipitation is not forecast again \nuntil Tuesday night, though 24 hr precipitation guidance only shows \na 10-20% chance for greater than 0.1 inches. However, this will \ndepend on the track and timing of the trough and surface low, which \nis relatively uncertain at this range. When looking at LREF members, \nENS and GEPS members favored a more northern track, while GEFS \nfavored a low coming through the Central Plains, which would be more \nsupportive of precipitation in the area. As such, a northern track \nis currently favored at around 60%.",
